# Movement 004 editorial review — chapters 23–29

## Review status and coverage

This is a fresh-context simulated editorial read with canon evidence, not a blind cold read and not a real human audience test. Coverage is the complete supplied movement: chapters 23–29, seven chapters, 36,707 total measured words (36,689 prose words and 18 words in the system notice). I also read the supplied edition brief, book map, state ledger through Movement 3, canon rules, universe bible, movement packet, numerical formula, and source-overlap summary. The movement is complete for its declared scope and reaches the required leaving state: the Brom bout is lost in four exchanges, Brom becomes a friend, Iron-adjacent arrives on the third sparring morning, the listening day establishes its first limits, and the main season is about to begin.

## Unscored reader response

Interest caught in the first page, where a body inventory turns into the two written prohibitions Cael must carry onto the main floor. The boarding-house egg, Lira's invented errands, twenty-six lamps, and the old yard-owner's double nod then make the bout feel like an event without replacing the personal question: can Cael obey his own rules when the plan fails? The strongest turn is not the unexplained disappearance itself but Cael's plain strike landing “with nothing in it but a boy,” followed by Brom choosing not to force the anomaly to happen again.

Interest slipped briefly at the opening of chapter 25. Chapter 24 has already rendered the first two exchanges and the disappearance in close detail; Brom's cutaway then summarizes the first two exchanges again before replaying the third from his side. The new information is excellent—especially the shelves, his body's reliance on the read, and his decision not to break what he cannot yet understand—but the approach to it repeats enough known action to delay the answer promised by the POV change. The later sparring sequence is long, but each morning changes the problem and the relationship, so it earns its space better.

Cael matters most, but Brom becomes the movement's emotional center: he wins without cruelty, offers equivalent information before asking for trust, accepts correction when he becomes overcareful, and teaches a capability whose logic runs against Cael's deepest habit. Lira remains essential because her care is neither effortless nor erased by the new friendship; “That was ours,” followed by her honest correction, gives her attachment and jealousy moral weight without making either shameful.

I expect the next movement to test Iron-adjacent in the noise of the main season, put strain on the new three-person relationship, and make the empty *Carrying* column matter beyond technique. I would continue without hesitation.

## Reader lenses

Scores are editorial judgments, not demographic findings. Confidence refers to confidence in the judgment from this seven-chapter sample.

| Dimension | Fluent thirteen-year-old | Adult genre reader | Exact location and reason | Confidence |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|
| Opening pull | 9 | 9 | Ch23 opening, from Cael's feet-up body check through “*Pressure: locked. Don't open it. Not once.*”: the body is sound, the danger is concrete, and the written rules promise a test before explanation takes over. | High |
| Keep reading | 9 | 10 | Ch24–25, the third-exchange disappearance and Brom's choice not to investigate it by driving Cael past his limits; ch29 then leaves a barely usable fragment, the main season, and Lira's unresolved judgment in play. | High |
| Interest / freshness | 9 | 10 | Ch24, “the next half-second had turned out to have a man in it whose read was pointed at an empty piece of floor,” and ch29, “It wanted him to be the floor”: the progression problem is built from attention, consent, and opposing ways of knowing rather than a simple power increase. | High |
| Clarity / flow | 8 | 8 | Ch24's exchange-by-exchange arithmetic and ch29's failed listening attempts are understandable on one pass. Ch25's recap of already-seen exchanges and some repeated knuckle/read explanations slow the line more than they confuse it. | High |
| Character attachment | 10 | 10 | Ch25, Brom sits beside Cael rather than over him; ch26, he tells the end of his story first; ch28, Cael hands over the whole Log; ch28, Lira admits “I minded.” Trust changes through costly choices on every side. | High |
| Humor / warmth | 9 | 9 | Ch23's coal-box egg and invented errands; ch27's quarter-strength test putting Cael on the floor; ch28's “*See the sheet*” laugh. The humor belongs to the people and releases tension without mocking pain. | High |
| Action / suspense | 9 | 10 | Ch24 from the first forearm strike through the fourth-exchange hand-up: terrain, breath, reach, knees, changing hypotheses, and the cost of restraint remain trackable. The outcome is a loss, but every exchange changes what both fighters know. | High |
| Progression payoff | 10 | 10 | Ch27–29: observation becomes touch knowledge, Cael asks for the full truth, Iron-adjacent arrives unbidden, and forty failures produce four breaths of listening. Capability is gained, priced, and shown to be nearly useless under combat conditions. | High |
| Connection | 10 | 10 | Ch23–29 carry the grey-book plan, Power Log rules, left-hip cost, Pressure hollow, Keth's request, Lira's lamp, Brom's limited read, Vell's wording, and the boarding-house relationships forward without dropping their consequences. | High |
| Read-aloud quality | 8 | 9 | Dialogue voices are distinct and attribution stays clear, including the three-person emotional triangle. Some long explanatory paragraphs in ch24–25 and ch27–29 repeat the same nouns—read, breath, hold, answer, cross—and ask sustained listening attention despite the very easy measured readability. | Medium-high |

## Continuity, canon, and story findings

### Result

No verified canon contradiction, reserved-truth breach, or broken power limit was found. The movement fulfills the packet's binding events in the required order. The substantive editorial finding is local pacing at the chapter 24/25 POV handoff, not a state or canon error.

### State, chronology, and physical continuity

- The bout occurs on the promised Tuesday after twelve days of observation. The opening carries Movement 3's state correctly: Cael's earlier hip, shoulder, rib, wrist, and knee marks have mostly faded; Lira's upper arm remains bruised from Wendel; the main floor is stone under a low ceiling and is lit by lamps; the old yard-owner appears only as a visitor.
- The price moves from four-to-one in Movement 3 to five-to-one on bout day because north-gate carters arrive. This is an explained market change, not a continuity error.
- The bout's four exchanges preserve position and damage. The first reactive Wind burst creates the wide hip line; the second, read-fed burst charges full price for one-third distance after both knees are hurt; Pressure is held shut twice and never deployed; the fourth exchange ends on the already-damaged knees. Cael raises his own hand, as promised to Dace.
- Recovery is plausible and carried rather than erased. Cael cannot stand after the bout, descends stairs seated the next morning, uses Lira's measuring stick, and reaches the market only slowly. On the fourth day he can manage the market steps unaided. The first two sparring mornings fix his feet on chalk crosses and preserve the stop-if-hurt term; full-strength returns wait until a week after the bout, when the knee still audibly reports the load.
